60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Option A is essentially the call-back trial expanded. It has proven efficacy in reducing readmissions. I vote for A.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option A is the only one that doesn't require more physical room or parking, as it moves the care to the patient's home.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option B is a ""joint"" venture between departments—a recipe for a second-year collapse. Option D relies on outside providers, which is a bureaucratic nightmare.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Option B! I saw that the midwifery team has some slack in their rota, so they could actually handle this without getting too stressed!","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Option A is the most prudent. It avoids the cost of new floor space and, by moving care off-site, it avoids the congestion of our halls.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Yumi, while the midwifery team may have capacity, we must prioritize the program that prevents hospital readmissions.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,I agree. Option C would be a disaster for our parking and booking system. Option A is the most absorbable.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"I find myself in agreement with the majority. Option A is the least likely to rely on a ""gentleman's agreement"" that will be ignored in twelve months.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"I see... if it helps the most people and doesn't make the ward nurses more tired, then I can support that too!","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,It is a rare and beautiful thing when we find a common path. Let us choose the way that is sustainable for all.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
